// Preload: what the language itself refers to, written in Rig. Every
// program imports it without an #import (spec 2.14): an ordinary program,
// a build program and each of its workspaces, rig check, a program the test
// mode runs interpreted (unit-tests --interpret), and the world of #run
// while compiling. rig finds it where it finds every module that ships with
// it, and reads this file right after the program's main file. In load
// order it comes first, before the main file (spec 2.13), so its
// declarations come first: a program that declares a name Preload declares
// is told so at its own declaration. Writing
//
//     #import "Preload";
//
// is allowed and reads nothing more, just as a #load of this file by its
// path reads nothing more: a file is read once.
//
// What Preload declares joins the program's one top level, so a program
// can't declare those names. README.md lists the ones that are for
// programs. Every name Preload declares for its own use starts with two
// underscores (__malloc, say), which leaves the plain names to programs:
// a convention, which the compiler doesn't enforce.

// The allocator: where memory comes from. An allocator is passed to
// whatever needs memory, rather than everything taking it from one global
// heap, so the caller decides where it comes from and how it goes back.
// Preload holds its shape, its modes and the heap, which every program
// has; Basic adds the helpers (alloc, resize, free and free_all), an arena
// and a pool.
//
// An allocator is Jai's shape: one procedure and the data it works on. The
// procedure is called with a mode, ALLOCATE, RESIZE, FREE or FREE_ALL, and
// does what the mode says, with the data as its last argument: an arena's
// procedure gets its Arena, a pool's its Pool, the heap's nothing. Anything
// that holds an Allocator can be given any of them, or one a program writes
// itself, without knowing which.

// The modes an allocator procedure is called with (constants until enums).
ALLOCATE :: 0;
RESIZE :: 1;
FREE :: 2;
FREE_ALL :: 3;

// Jai's shape: one procedure and its data. The procedure type is written
// out, since Rig has no type aliases yet.
//
// The procedure is called as proc(mode, size, old_size, old_memory, data):
//
// - ALLOCATE: size bytes, or null.
// - RESIZE: old_memory, which holds old_size bytes, made size bytes long,
//   moved or not, with the first of its old bytes kept; or null, and then
//   old_memory is as it was.
// - FREE: gives old_memory back. Returns null.
// - FREE_ALL: gives back everything the allocator gave. Returns null.
Allocator :: struct {
    proc: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void;
    data: *void;
}

// C's heap, malloc, realloc and free, declared under Preload's own names
// (#foreign libc "NAME", spec 7.6), so that a program keeps C's: it may
// declare C's malloc, realloc or free itself, and its free and __free are
// the same C function.
__malloc :: (size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "malloc";
__realloc :: (memory: *void, size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "realloc";
__free :: (memory: *void) #foreign libc "free";

// The heap: C's malloc, realloc and free, through Preload's own
// declarations. Its memory is aligned for any type, as C's is.
//
// FREE_ALL does nothing: the heap doesn't know what it gave, so each piece
// goes back with FREE. A size above 0 converts to C's size_t, a u64,
// unchanged. The procedure asks C for exactly what it's given: Basic's
// helpers never ask it for 0 bytes, whose result C leaves to each C
// library, nor give it null to resize or free.
heap_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    if mode == ALLOCATE { return __malloc(cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == RESIZE { return __realloc(old_memory, cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == FREE { __free(old_memory); }
    return null;
}

// The heap as an Allocator. It needs no data.
HEAP_ALLOCATOR :: Allocator.{heap_allocator_proc, null};

// Type information (spec 3.16): Jai's, trimmed to Rig's types.
// type_info(T) (spec 5.19) gives a *Type_Info, the address of a
// description of T laid out as the structs below, and an Any pairs a
// value's address with its type's description. The compiler writes each
// description, once per type, in the program's read-only data (spec 10.3),
// and in the interpreter's memory while compiling (spec 10.8), laid out as
// these structs are: src/types.h mirrors them. A description is read-only:
// writing one is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6).
//
// Every description starts with a Type_Info, whose tag says its kind. Each
// kind's struct holds that Type_Info as its first field, info (Rig has no
// using), so a *Type_Info whose tag says the kind is cast to the kind's
// struct: cast(*Type_Info_Struct) t when t.tag == TYPE_INFO_STRUCT.

// The kinds, a Type_Info's tag (constants until enums), numbered as Jai's
// tags are.
TYPE_INFO_INTEGER :: 0;
TYPE_INFO_FLOAT :: 1;
TYPE_INFO_BOOL :: 2;
TYPE_INFO_STRING :: 3;
TYPE_INFO_POINTER :: 4;
TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE :: 5;
TYPE_INFO_VOID :: 6;
TYPE_INFO_STRUCT :: 7;
TYPE_INFO_ARRAY :: 8;

// What every description starts with: its kind, then the type's size and
// alignment in bytes (spec 3.11; void's are 0 and 1). A float, bool,
// string or void (TYPE_INFO_FLOAT, _BOOL, _STRING, _VOID) is described by
// this alone: a float's size says which it is.
Type_Info :: struct { tag: int; size: int; alignment: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_INTEGER: s8 to u64, and int. int and s64 are two types with
// two descriptions, the same in all but their addresses.
Type_Info_Integer :: struct { info: Type_Info; signed: bool; }

// TYPE_INFO_POINTER: *T, and the description of the T it points at
// (void's for a *void).
Type_Info_Pointer :: struct { info: Type_Info; pointer_to: *Type_Info; }

// TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE: a procedure type, its parameters' types and its
// results', in order, whether it's called as C calls (#c_call), and whether
// its last parameter is a ..T, whose type is then described as []T.
Type_Info_Procedure :: struct {
    info: Type_Info;
    parameter_types: []*Type_Info;
    result_types: []*Type_Info;
    is_c_call: bool;
    is_variadic: bool;
}

// TYPE_INFO_STRUCT: a struct, by its declared name, and its fields in
// order, none for a struct with none. A struct that holds a pointer to
// itself is described by pointing back at its own description: the data is
// cyclic, not infinite.
Type_Info_Struct :: struct { info: Type_Info; name: string; members: []Type_Info_Struct_Member; }

// One field of a struct: its name, its type's description, and where it is
// in the struct, in bytes from its start (spec 3.9).
Type_Info_Struct_Member :: struct { name: string; type: *Type_Info; offset: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_ARRAY: an array, [N]T, or a view, []T, which array_kind tells
// apart (ARRAY_FIXED or ARRAY_VIEW); the description of T; and N, or -1 for
// a view. A string is no view here: it has its own tag, TYPE_INFO_STRING.
Type_Info_Array :: struct { info: Type_Info; element_type: *Type_Info; array_kind: int; count: int; }

// A Type_Info_Array's array_kind.
ARRAY_FIXED :: 0;
ARRAY_VIEW :: 1;

// A value of any type: its type's description and its address. Every value
// converts to an Any implicitly (spec 3.16), a place by its own address, any
// other value by a temporary copy's, which lasts to the end of the
// statement; an Any converts as itself. It is an ordinary struct, so it has
// a description of its own, a struct named Any. The program reads the value
// by casting value_pointer once type says what it is.
Any :: struct { type: *Type_Info; value_pointer: *void; }
